  "whyConfessTitle": "Why Confess?",
  "whyConfessContent": "Confession is a Sacrament instituted by Jesus Christ to reconcile us with God and with the Church. It is a personal encounter with divine mercy.\n\n**Spiritual Importance:**\nConfession restores the sanctifying grace lost by mortal sin and increases grace in our soul. It strengthens our will against future temptations and gives us peace of conscience.\n\n**Temporal Importance:**\nConfessing our mistakes frees us from the weight of guilt, improves our relationships, and helps us live a more authentic and full life. The humility required to confess makes us better people.",
  "psychBenefitsTitle": "Psychological Benefits",
  "psychBenefitsContent": "Scientific studies demonstrate various psychological benefits of confession:\n\n**Stress Reduction:**\nKeeping secrets and feelings of guilt increases cortisol levels. Confessing relieves this emotional burden.\n\n**Improved Mental Health:**\nThe regular practice of examining conscience and confessing promotes self-knowledge and personal growth.\n\n**Overcoming Addictions:**\nAdmitting our mistakes is the first step towards change. Confession creates accountability and motivation to improve.\n\n**Healthier Relationships:**\nHonesty with oneself facilitates more authentic and deeper relationships with others.",
  "saintsWordsTitle": "Words of the Saints",
  "saintsWordsContent": "\"He who confesses with tears receives forgiveness with joy.\"\n— St. John Mary Vianney (Curé of Ars)\n\n\"Confession is an act of honesty and courage — an act of entrusting oneself, beyond words, to the merciful Lord.\"\n— Pope Saint John Paul II\n\n\"Through confession, man looks the truth of his sins in the face and takes responsibility for them; thereby, he opens himself anew to God.\"\n— Pope Benedict XVI\n\n\"Oh! How great is the goodness of our Lord! In reality, He forgets our sins after confession.\"\n— St. Faustina Kowalska\n\n\"Confession is not to be judged, but to be forgiven.\"\n— St. Padre Pio of Pietrelcina",
  "tipsTitle": "Tips for the Anxious and Shy",
  "tipsContent": "If you feel anxiety or shame about confessing:\n\n1. **Prepare in advance:** Write your sins on a piece of paper. You can bring this paper to the confessional.\n\n2. **Remember: The priest is not a judge:** He is there to forgive and counsel, not to judge you.\n\n3. **The priest has heard it all:** Your sin is probably not unique or exceptional. Nothing will shock him.\n\n4. **Start with the \"easier\" sins:** This can help build courage for the more difficult ones.\n\n5. **Focus on God, not the priest:** You are confessing to Jesus Christ, through the priest.\n\n6. **Trust in mercy:** God already knows all your sins and yet loves you infinitely.\n\n7. **Go frequently:** The more you confess, the easier it becomes. Practice brings comfort.",
  "secrecyTitle": "The Sacramental Seal",
  "secrecyContent": "The Seal of Confession is ABSOLUTE and INVIOLABLE.\n\nThe priest is prohibited, under penalty of automatic excommunication, from revealing ANYTHING he has heard in confession. This includes:\n\n• He cannot tell anyone, not even another priest.\n• He cannot use the information in any way.\n• He cannot give hints that he knows something.\n• Not even under torture or threat of death can he break the seal.\n\n**Historical Cases:**\nSeveral martyr priests preferred death over breaking the sacramental seal, such as St. John Nepomucene, martyred in 1393 for refusing to reveal the queen's confession.\n\n**Your confession is safe.** Not even God would allow the seal to be broken, protecting this precious Sacrament.",
  "priestAttitudeTitle": "The Attitude of the Priest",
  "priestAttitudeContent": "The priest will maintain the same welcoming attitude, no matter the sin.\n\n**Why?**\n• The priest acts \"in Persona Christi\" — representing Christ.\n• Christ welcomed everyone: prostitutes, thieves, murderers.\n• The priest is trained not to show shock or judgment.\n• He knows we are all sinners seeking mercy.\n\n**What the priest thinks:**\nInstead of judging, the priest thinks:\n• \"How can I help this person?\"\n• \"What advice can I give to avoid future falls?\"\n• \"I praise God for the courage of this soul in seeking reconciliation.\"\n\n**Your experience:**\nRegardless of what you confess, you will be received with:\n• Respect and dignity.\n• Compassion and mercy.\n• Guidance and encouragement.\n• Absolution in the name of Jesus Christ.\n\n**Remember:** The priest is there as an instrument of God's mercy, not as a judge. His function is to forgive and help, not condemn.",
