  "massGuideTitle": "Guide to the Mass",
  "massGuideSubtitle": "Follow each part of the celebration.",
  "massGuideTipsTitle": "Before you go",
  "massGuideTipsBody": "We arrive a few minutes early, silence our phones, and make the Sign of the Cross with holy water upon entering the church. At our pew, we genuflect (bending the right knee to the floor) toward the tabernacle, where Jesus is present in the Eucharist, and spend a moment in silent preparation.\n\nTo receive Communion, we must be in a state of grace: if we are conscious of grave sin, we go to Confession first. We keep the one-hour Eucharistic fast before Communion (water and medicine do not break the fast). Mass on Sundays and holy days of obligation is binding.",
  "massGuidePart1Title": "1. Introductory Rites",
  "massGuidePart1Body": "Posture: Stand throughout the Introductory Rites.\n\nEntrance procession and song: as the priest approaches and kisses the altar, the assembly welcomes the start of the celebration.\n\nSign of the Cross and greeting: we make the Sign of the Cross with the priest and respond:\n» The Lord be with you.\n» — And with your spirit.\n\nPenitential Act: we pause briefly to acknowledge our sins and ask God's mercy. When the general confession is prayed, we gently strike our breast when acknowledging our fault. The invocations of mercy follow:\n» Lord, have mercy. / Christ, have mercy.\n\nGlory to God in the highest: sung or recited on Sundays (outside Advent and Lent), solemnities, and feasts, praising the Most Holy Trinity.\n\nOpening Prayer (Collect): at the invitation \"Let us pray,\" we pause briefly in silence to unite our personal intentions. At the end of the priest's prayer, all respond: Amen.",
  "massGuidePart2Title": "2. Liturgy of the Word",
  "massGuidePart2Body": "Posture: Sit for the readings, the Psalm, and the homily; stand for the Gospel Acclamation, the Gospel, the Creed, and the Prayer of the Faithful.\n\nReadings and Responsorial Psalm: we listen to the first reading, respond to the Psalm, and on Sundays and solemnities hear the second reading. After each reading:\n» The word of the Lord.\n» — Thanks be to God.\n\nGospel Acclamation and Gospel: we stand to acclaim the Gospel. At the announcement \"A reading from the holy Gospel...\", we respond \"Glory to you, O Lord\" while tracing a small sign of the cross with your thumb on your forehead (to understand), lips (to proclaim), and chest (to keep in our hearts). At the end:\n» The Gospel of the Lord.\n» — Praise to you, Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nHomily: seated, we listen to the explanation of God's Word applied to Christian life.\n\nProfession of Faith (Creed): standing on Sundays and solemnities, we profess the faith of the Church. At the words recalling the Incarnation of Christ, we make a profound bow (and genuflect on the solemnities of the Annunciation and Christmas).\n\nPrayer of the Faithful: remaining standing, we present petitions for the Church, the world, and those in need, answering each intention (for example: \"Lord, hear our prayer\").",
  "massGuidePart3Title": "3. Preparation of the Gifts",
  "massGuidePart3Body": "Posture: Sit while the altar is prepared and the gifts are brought forward; stand at the invitation \"Pray, brethren.\"\n\nPresentation of bread and wine: the gifts of bread and wine are placed upon the altar during the offertory song or in silence. We offer to God our daily work, joys, trials, and intentions along with the gifts.\n\nWashing of hands: the priest washes his hands as a sign of interior purification before the Eucharistic Sacrifice.\n\nInvitation to prayer and Prayer over the Offerings: we stand when the priest invites the assembly to pray:\n» Pray, brethren...\n» — May the Lord accept the sacrifice at your hands for the praise and glory of his name, for our good and the good of all his holy Church.\n\nAt the end of the Prayer over the Offerings, all respond: Amen.",
  "massGuidePart4Title": "4. Eucharistic Prayer and Consecration",
  "massGuidePart4Body": "Posture: Stand for the Preface dialogue and the Holy, Holy, Holy; kneel from the invocation of the Holy Spirit over the gifts until the acclamation after the Consecration (or throughout the Eucharistic Prayer, according to local custom).\n\nPreface dialogue: opens the great prayer of thanksgiving:\n» The Lord be with you. — And with your spirit.\n» Lift up your hearts. — We lift them up to the Lord.\n» Let us give thanks to the Lord our God. — It is right and just.\n\nHoly, Holy, Holy: we unite with the angels and saints in proclaiming \"Holy, Holy, Holy...\"\n\nConsecration and Elevation: we kneel in deep silence. The priest invokes the Holy Spirit and speaks the words of Jesus at the Last Supper; the bread and wine truly become the Body and Blood of Christ. When the priest elevates the Consecrated Host and the Chalice, we look upon them with faith and adore silently (we may pray inwardly: \"My Lord and my God!\").\n\nMemorial Acclamation, intercessions, and Great Amen: at \"The mystery of faith,\" we acclaim Christ's death and resurrection. The Church intercedes for the Pope, the bishops, the living, and the dead. At the final elevation (\"Through him, and with him, and in him...\"), all affirm with the Great Amen.",
  "massGuidePart5Title": "5. Communion Rite",
  "massGuidePart5Body": "Posture: Stand for the Lord's Prayer, Sign of Peace, and Lamb of God; kneel (or stand reverently, according to local custom) before Communion; kneel or sit in silent thanksgiving after receiving; stand for the Prayer after Communion.\n\nThe Lord's Prayer and Sign of Peace: we pray the Our Father together. Then the priest offers the peace of Christ:\n» The peace of the Lord be with you always.\n» — And with your spirit.\nWhen invited, we offer a sober, respectful sign of peace to those nearby.\n\nBreaking of the Bread and Lamb of God: while the priest breaks the Host, we sing or recite the \"Lamb of God,\" asking for mercy and peace.\n\nImmediate preparation: when the priest holds up the consecrated Host (\"Behold the Lamb of God...\"), we respond:\n» Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but only say the word and my soul shall be healed.\n\nHoly Communion: as we approach, we make a gesture of reverence (a bow of the head or genuflection) before receiving. When the minister says \"The Body of Christ,\" we reply with faith: Amen. Those unable to receive Communion may remain recollected and make a Spiritual Communion.\n\nThanksgiving: back at our pew, we spend quiet moments thanking Jesus present within us. Then we stand for the Prayer after Communion, concluding with: Amen.",
  "massGuidePart6Title": "6. Concluding Rites",
  "massGuidePart6Body": "Posture: Sit during parish announcements (if any); stand for the final blessing and dismissal.\n\nGreeting and Final Blessing: the priest greets the people again (\"The Lord be with you\" — \"And with your spirit\") and imparts the blessing in the name of the Most Holy Trinity. We make the Sign of the Cross as we receive the blessing and respond: Amen.\n\nDismissal: we are sent forth to live the Gospel in daily life:\n» Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life.\n» — Thanks be to God.\n\nDeparture: we remain in place until the priest venerates the altar and departs. Before leaving the church, we take a brief moment of thanksgiving and genuflect when passing before the tabernacle.",
  "adorationTitle": "Eucharistic Adoration",
  "adorationSubtitle": "An hour before Jesus in the Blessed Sacrament.",
  "adorationStep1Title": "1. When you enter",
  "adorationStep1Body": "Make the sign of the cross, genuflect before the Blessed Sacrament (exposed or in the tabernacle) and find a quiet place.",
  "adorationStep2Title": "2. Be silent",
  "adorationStep2Body": "Remain before Jesus without hurry. You do not need to say much: look at Him and let Him look at you.",
  "adorationStep3Title": "3. Pray",
  "adorationStep3Body": "Some prayers that can help:",
  "adorationStep4Title": "4. Speak with Jesus",
  "adorationStep4Body": "Give thanks, ask forgiveness and present your intentions and those of the people you love. If you wish, read the day's Gospel.",
  "adorationStep5Title": "5. When you leave",
  "adorationStep5Body": "Give thanks, make the sign of the cross, genuflect and carry the peace you received into your day.",
