  "massGuideFormTitle": "Form of the Mass",
  "massGuideFormOrdinary": "Ordinary Form (vernacular)",
  "massGuideFormOrdinaryDesc": "Today's Mass, celebrated in the language of the people.",
  "massGuideFormTraditional": "Traditional Latin Mass",
  "massGuideFormTraditionalDesc": "The Mass celebrated with the 1962 Roman Missal, in Latin.",
  "massGuideTradSubtitle": "Follow the Traditional Latin Mass.",
  "massGuideTradTipsTitle": "Before you go",
  "massGuideTradTipsBody": "The Traditional Mass is celebrated with the 1962 Roman Missal. Find out in your diocese where it is celebrated. Bring a bilingual missal (Latin and your language) to follow along. It is customary to keep silence in the church, for women to cover their heads with a veil, and to receive Communion kneeling and on the tongue. Much of the prayer is said quietly by the priest: participation is above all interior. The rules on fasting and being in a state of grace to receive Communion are the same.",
  "massGuideTradPart1Title": "1. At the foot of the altar",
  "massGuideTradPart1Body": "The priest and ministers prepare with the sign of the cross and Psalm 42. Then come the Confiteor (confession of sins) and the plea for pardon.\n\n» In nomine Patris, et Filii, et Spiritus Sancti. Amen.\n» Introibo ad altare Dei.\n» Ad Deum qui laetificat juventutem meam.\nI will go in to the altar of God. — To God, who gives joy to my youth.",
  "massGuideTradPart2Title": "2. Introit, Kyrie and Gloria",
  "massGuideTradPart2Body": "The Introit is the entrance chant. The Kyrie, a plea for mercy, follows and, on Sundays outside Advent and Lent, the Gloria. The Collect is the prayer of the day.\n\n» Kyrie, eleison. Christe, eleison. Kyrie, eleison.\nLord, have mercy. Christ, have mercy. Lord, have mercy.\n\n» Dominus vobiscum.\n» Et cum spiritu tuo.\nThe Lord be with you. — And with your spirit.\n\n» Oremus. (…) — Amen.",
  "massGuideTradPart3Title": "3. Epistle, Gradual and Gospel",
  "massGuideTradPart3Body": "The Epistle is read, the Gradual (and Alleluia) is sung and the Gospel is proclaimed, heard standing. It is customary to make three small signs of the cross on the forehead, lips and heart.\n\n» Deo gratias.\nThanks be to God. (after the Epistle)\n\n» Dominus vobiscum. — Et cum spiritu tuo.\n» Sequentia sancti Evangelii secundum N.\n» Gloria tibi, Domine.\nThe continuation of the holy Gospel according to N. — Glory to you, O Lord.\n\n» Laus tibi, Christe.\nPraise to you, O Christ. (after the Gospel)",
  "massGuideTradPart4Title": "4. Sermon, Creed and Offertory",
  "massGuideTradPart4Body": "After the sermon, on Sundays and feasts, the Creed is said (genuflect at \"et incarnatus est\"). At the Offertory the priest offers the bread and wine.\n\n» Credo in unum Deum, Patrem omnipotentem…\nI believe in one God, the Father almighty…\n\n» Orate, fratres: ut meum ac vestrum sacrificium acceptabile fiat apud Deum Patrem omnipotentem.\n» Suscipiat Dominus sacrificium de manibus tuis ad laudem et gloriam nominis sui, ad utilitatem quoque nostram, totiusque Ecclesiae suae sanctae.\nPray, brethren, that my sacrifice and yours may be acceptable to God the Father almighty. — May the Lord receive the sacrifice at your hands, to the praise and glory of his name, for our benefit and that of all his holy Church.",
  "massGuideTradPart5Title": "5. Preface, Sanctus and Canon",
  "massGuideTradPart5Body": "The Preface opens the great prayer of thanksgiving. After the Sanctus, the priest prays the Canon silently. At the consecration, kneeling, we adore Jesus present in the Host and the Chalice, at the sound of the bell.\n\n» Sursum corda.\n» Habemus ad Dominum.\n» Gratias agamus Domino Deo nostro.\n» Dignum et justum est.\nLift up your hearts. — We lift them up to the Lord. — Let us give thanks to the Lord our God. — It is right and just.\n\n» Sanctus, Sanctus, Sanctus Dominus Deus Sabaoth. Pleni sunt caeli et terra gloria tua. Hosanna in excelsis. Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini. Hosanna in excelsis.\nHoly, Holy, Holy, Lord God of hosts. Heaven and earth are full of your glory. Hosanna in the highest. Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. Hosanna in the highest.",
  "massGuideTradPart6Title": "6. Our Father, Agnus Dei and Communion",
  "massGuideTradPart6Body": "The priest sings the Our Father, and the people answer at the end. The Agnus Dei follows. Before Communion, the prayer of humility is repeated three times. Communion is received kneeling, at the altar rail.\n\n» Et ne nos inducas in tentationem.\n» Sed libera nos a malo.\nAnd lead us not into temptation. — But deliver us from evil.\n\n» Agnus Dei, qui tollis peccata mundi: miserere nobis. (twice)\n» Agnus Dei, qui tollis peccata mundi: dona nobis pacem.\nLamb of God, who takes away the sins of the world, have mercy on us. (twice) — Lamb of God, who takes away the sins of the world, grant us peace.\n\n» Domine, non sum dignus ut intres sub tectum meum: sed tantum dic verbo, et sanabitur anima mea. (three times)\nLord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but only say the word and my soul shall be healed.",
  "massGuideTradPart7Title": "7. Concluding Rites and Last Gospel",
  "massGuideTradPart7Body": "After the closing prayer, the priest dismisses the people and gives the blessing. The Mass ends with the reading of the Prologue of Saint John, the Last Gospel.\n\n» Ite, missa est.\n» Deo gratias.\nGo, the Mass is ended. — Thanks be to God.\n\n» Benedicat vos omnipotens Deus, Pater, et Filius, et Spiritus Sanctus.\n» Amen.\nMay almighty God bless you, the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit. — Amen.\n\n» Et Verbum caro factum est, et habitavit in nobis.\nAnd the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us. (genuflect)",
